[rvn-ple] Key shared PLE tables to a checkpoint tag; unpin before unmap

Patch 0060 names a shared packed PLE table by role + TP rank + shape + dtype
and nothing else, so any two checkpoints that share a padded shard shape share
a filename. A loader pointed at a different same-shape checkpoint -- a
re-converted candidate, a retired source rebuilt, a checkpoint belonging to
someone else on the same host -- therefore opens the file a live replica is
gathering rows out of and rewrites it in place, and neither side sees an error:
the rows the running model dereferences become another model's bytes while both
replicas keep serving. The filename now carries the checkpoint as well. Each
shared table is named with the first 16 hex chars of sha256 over an identity
built from what the loader itself consumes: the served model path and the
rvn_mtp_graft stamp (source, encoder_version, count) read from that checkpoint's
own config.json -- the same stamp patch 0058 gates a grafted draft on. One
checkpoint derives one tag, so a replica pair still converges on exactly one
file and the alias check still runs; a different checkpoint derives a different
tag and lands on its own file; a process whose serving identity is unknown (no
serving config, unreadable config, unstamped checkpoint) declines the shared
path rather than leaving an anonymous table behind. Attaching now also refuses a
shared directory that already holds a table whose tag is not this checkpoint's,
the untagged pre-0061 names included: those files are named by no identity, and
a foreign-named table is refused, never rewritten -- not opened, truncated or
unlinked here -- so the foreign checkpoint gets its own file or the private
pinned table.

Second, the pin. Between a successful cudaHostRegister and the line that
publishes the mapping as serving, 0060 has calls that can raise -- the alias
query itself, an interrupted torch.frombuffer, a dtype or view error -- and its
handler only logged and returned, leaving the registration pinned inside a
mapping the process no longer tracked while the fallback pinned a private copy
on top of it: host RAM consumed twice per table and a pin held over memory
nothing owns. Registration success is now held in a flag, and a finally block
runs on every path that does not end in a serving mapping, releases the pin
first and closes the mapping only afterwards, through the one handle that took
it, and only where a pin was actually taken -- a failed registration is not
unregistered. The fallback to the private pinned table is one path with one log
line, so no failure can both share and privately allocate.

The runtime is resolved from /proc/self/maps and counted there by file identity
(device and inode), because the pin, the device-alias query and the unpin have
to be answered by one and the same libcudart for the alias to mean anything.
0060 took the pin through one library and asked for the alias through
CDLL("libcudart.so"): in this image that name binds
/usr/local/cuda-13.0/targets/x86_64-linux/lib/libcudart.so.13.0.96 while the
runtime actually mapped in the process and used for device calls is
/usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages/nvidia/cu13/lib/libcudart.so.13, so the
alias that came back described a different instance from the pin, and the
unregister went off to a third path. Only the exact mapped path is handed to
CDLL now -- no bare soname, no toolkit copy -- and anything other than exactly
one mapped runtime, including none, declines the shared mapping instead of
guessing. RTLD_LOCAL replaces RTLD_GLOBAL, so the runtime's symbols are no
longer published into the global namespace for other components to bind.

Those calls are declared before they are used: cudaHostRegister and
cudaHostUnregister take a pointer, a size_t and a flags int with an int return,
and cudaHostGetDevicePointer takes a pointer, a pointer and an unsigned int with
an int return. Without argtypes ctypes passes every integer as a C int, and the
production rows table is 25,600,122,880 bytes -- past 2**31 and past 2**32 -- so
a silently truncated size, or a size that cannot be converted at all, would
surface as a failed shared mapping and a second private pin rather than as
anything a log would show. Assigning the signatures also resolves the symbols,
so a runtime missing any of the three is refused before anything is pinned, and
a size the runtime cannot express is refused too.

With SGLANG_PLE_SHARED_DIR unset the allocation path is byte-for-byte the one
this chain shipped before patch 0060: no new import, no new file, no new call.

